Track 3: The Low Tide Erases the Provincial Border
- Story & Context: Years after land reclamation dikes dried out the original floating market, retired tugboat operator Old Man Bae claimed that deep navigation buoys in the Han River Estuary Buffer Zone still resonate with phantom radio music at 5:00 AM. Local crate-diggers discovered that the market grandmothers had left a solar-powered tape machine encased in beeswax aboard an unmanned wooden marker buoy, playing an unending tape loop of slow-ascending cymbal swells and jangly clean guitar chords that only powers up when the first light hits the salt marshes. Fishermen cutting their diesel engines in the morning fog frequently hear the spectral hook echoing off the concrete sea wall: "We traded all our compasses for tea and a slower snare." The track stands as the genre's enduring myth, cementing how a cluster of market boats permanently rewrote the emotional geography of the waterway.
